{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778377577764,"sessionID":"ses_1f06fc8eeffeeG8U37JKhOluDB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_tJUc6OwSNqosUHn6CIGsQ2iA","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"jq -r '.body' '__issue_context__.json'","timeout":120000,"workdir":"/__w/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl","description":"Prints full imported issue body"},"output":"**CL term**\n- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \n- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \n\nThese classes are currently used with *in taxon: Homo sapiens*.\n\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\nFor both parent classes (CL_0001204 and CL_0001203), please remove:\n1. **CD44-high**  \n2. **CD122-high**\nRemoving these markers from the parent classes will correctly update all subclasses inheriting from these CD45RO-positive memory T cell terms.\n\n**Improved definitions**\n**- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive **\n\nA CD4-positive, alpha-beta long-lived T cell with the phenotype CD45RO-positive and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\n\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224 \n\n\n**- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  **\n\nA CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell with memory phenotype indicated by being CD45RO and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\n\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224 \n\n\n\n**Rationale**\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\n\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-positive memory T cells**\nExpression of IL-2Rβ (CD122) is variable across human memory T-cell subsets. High expression of CD122 is a feature of T stem cell memory (T_SCM) and certain innate/virtual memory–like subsets, but not of all CD45RO-positive memory T cells such as: central memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_CM)effector memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_EM)Thus, enforcing CD122-high at the parent level over constrains the phenotype of typical human memory T cells.\n\n**3. The current CD44-high and CD122-high constraints originate from mouse memory T cell biology**\nCD44-high (and, in some contexts, CD122-high) is widely used in mouse memory-like CD8⁺ T-cell phenotyping. However, these markers do not define human memory T-cell subsets. Removing these axioms will make the human memory T-cell hierarchy more accurate and species-appropriate.\n\nFor these reasons, removing CD44-high and CD122-high from the parent classes CL_0001204 and CL_0001203 will make the human memory T cell hierarchy more accurate and biologically appropriate.\n\n**References**  \n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224  *Table 4 includes the markers used to identify memory T-cell subsets.\n\n","metadata":{"output":"**CL term**\n- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \n- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  \n\nThese classes are currently used with *in taxon: Homo sapiens*.\n\n**Suggested revision of class hierarchy**\nFor both parent classes (CL_0001204 and CL_0001203), please remove:\n1. **CD44-high**  \n2. **CD122-high**\nRemoving these markers from the parent classes will correctly update all subclasses inheriting from these CD45RO-positive memory T cell terms.\n\n**Improved definitions**\n**- [CL_0001204](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001204) — CD4-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive **\n\nA CD4-positive, alpha-beta long-lived T cell with the phenotype CD45RO-positive and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\n\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224 \n\n\n**- [CL_0001203](http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0001203) — CD8-positive, alpha-beta memory T cell, CD45RO-positive  **\n\nA CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell with memory phenotype indicated by being CD45RO and CD127-positive. This cell type is also described as being CD25-negative.\n\n**References- do not replace existing references but add these along existing ones**\n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224 \n\n\n\n**Rationale**\n**1. CD44-high is not a defining marker for human memory T cells**\nIn human peripheral blood, CD44 is expressed at high levels on most CD4⁺ and CD8⁺ T cells, including naïve, central memory, and effector memory populations.  \nTherefore, CD44-high is not used to distinguish memory subsets in humans.CD44-based naïve vs memory separation is specific to mouse immunophenotyping.\n\n**2. CD122-high is characteristic of specific memory-like subsets, not all CD45RO-positive memory T cells**\nExpression of IL-2Rβ (CD122) is variable across human memory T-cell subsets. High expression of CD122 is a feature of T stem cell memory (T_SCM) and certain innate/virtual memory–like subsets, but not of all CD45RO-positive memory T cells such as: central memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_CM)effector memory CD4/CD8 T cells (T_EM)Thus, enforcing CD122-high at the parent level over constrains the phenotype of typical human memory T cells.\n\n**3. The current CD44-high and CD122-high constraints originate from mouse memory T cell biology**\nCD44-high (and, in some contexts, CD122-high) is widely used in mouse memory-like CD8⁺ T-cell phenotyping. However, these markers do not define human memory T-cell subsets. Removing these axioms will make the human memory T-cell hierarchy more accurate and species-appropriate.\n\nFor these reasons, removing CD44-high and CD122-high from the parent classes CL_0001204 and CL_0001203 will make the human memory T cell hierarchy more accurate and biologically appropriate.\n\n**References**  \n- PMID: 24258910\n- PMID: 21926977  \n- PMID: 41254224  *Table 4 includes the markers used to identify memory T-cell subsets.\n\n","exit":0,"description":"Prints full imported issue body","truncated":false},"title":"Prints full imported issue body","time":{"start":1778377577758,"end":1778377577763}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_099c832caedff5af0169ffe3695a70819d80eede53ae10da92"}},"id":"prt_e0f9053c3001zZfd90uBrroevJ","sessionID":"ses_1f06fc8eeffeeG8U37JKhOluDB","messageID":"msg_e0f9045ed001mwwz3Jx2s9okBn"}}
