tion: human memory T-cell subsets\n\n[Yolanda D Mahnke](/?term=Mahnke+YD&cauthor_id=24258910)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Translational and Correlative Studies Laboratory, Abramson Family Cancer Research Center, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, PA, USA.\") , [Tess M Brodie](/?term=Brodie+TM&cauthor_id=24258910), [Federica Sallusto](/?term=Sallusto+F&cauthor_id=24258910), [Mario Roederer](/?term=Roederer+M&cauthor_id=24258910), [Enrico Lugli](/?term=Lugli+E&cauthor_id=24258910)\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Translational and Correlative Studies Laboratory, Abramson Family Cancer Research Center, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, PA, USA.\n\n-   PMID: **24258910**\n-   DOI: [10.1002/eji.201343751](https://doi.org/10.1002/eji.201343751)\n\nFree article\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nReview\n\n# The who's who of T-cell differentiation: human memory T-cell subsets\n\nYolanda D Mahnke et al. Eur J Immunol. 2013 Nov.\n\nFree article\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nEur J Immunol\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Eur+J+Immunol%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Eur+J+Immunol%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2013 Nov;43(11):2797-809.\n\ndoi: 10.1002/eji.201343751. Epub 2013 Oct 30.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Yolanda D Mahnke](/?term=Mahnke+YD&cauthor_id=24258910)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Translational and Correlative Studies Laboratory, Abramson Family Cancer Research Center, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, PA, USA.\") , [Tess M Brodie](/?term=Brodie+TM&cauthor_id=24258910), [Federica Sallusto](/?term=Sallusto+F&cauthor_id=24258910), [Mario Roederer](/?term=Roederer+M&cauthor_id=24258910), [Enrico Lugli](/?term=Lugli+E&cauthor_id=24258910)\n\n### Affiliation\n\n-   1 Translational and Correlative Studies Laboratory, Abramson Family Cancer Research Center, Perelman School of Medicine, University of Pennsylvania, PA, USA.\n\n-   PMID: **24258910**\n-   DOI: [10.1002/eji.201343751](https://doi.org/10.1002/eji.201343751)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nFollowing antigen encounter and subsequent resolution of the immune response, a single naïve T cell is able to generate multiple subsets of memory T cells with different phenotypic and functional properties and gene expression profiles. Single-cell technologies, first and foremost flow cytometry, have revealed the complex heterogeneity of the memory T-cell compartment and its organization into subsets. However, a consensus has still to be reached, both at the semantic (nomenclature) and phenotypic level, regarding the identification of these subsets. Here, we review recent developments in the characterization of the heterogeneity of the memory T-cell compartment, and propose a unified classification of both human and nonhuman primate T cells on the basis of phenotypic traits and in vivo properties. Given that vaccine studies and adoptive cell transfer immunotherapy protocols are influenced by these recent findings, it is important to use uniform methods for identifying and discussing functionally distinct subsets of T cells.\n\n**Keywords:** Flow cytometry; Human T-cell subsets; Memory T-cell differentiation; Naïve T cells.\n\n© 2013 WILEY-VCH Verlag GmbH & Co. KGaA, Weinheim.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Research Support, N.I.H., Intramural\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Intramural%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Research+Support%2C+N.I.H.%2C+Intramural)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search 